# Answer 3 · The Discipline of Gender Studies, and Women's Studies versus Gender Studies (CSS 2017)
@py question 2017-2

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Three tasks: define the discipline, discuss it (scope, aims, why it arose, why it matters), then differentiate it from Women's Studies.
@ask ① DEFINE the discipline of Gender Studies
@ask ② DISCUSS it — scope, aims, significance
@ask ③ DIFFERENTIATE it from Women's Studies
(1) Introduction: sex is biology, gender is society; Scott: gender is a way of showing power; the two fields are partners
(2) Definition: studies how society makes gender; how gender shares out power; uses many disciplines
(3) Scope: family, work, politics, law, culture, violence; women, men and gender-diverse people
(4) Why it arose: knowledge treated men as the norm; GDP ignored women's unpaid work; "add women and stir" failed
(5) Aims and features: explain inequality, guide policy, empower; questions who makes knowledge
(6) Significance: needed for CEDAW and SDG 5 reporting; Pakistan 143rd of 145 in gender gap
(7) Women's Studies: began at Cornell 1969 and San Diego 1970; puts women at the centre
(8) The differences: WS studies women; GS studies relations, including men; GS adds new theories
(9) Examples: honour killing and maternal health seen through both lenses
(10) Conclusion: GS completes WS; women must not disappear
:::

## Decoding the question
Three command words. **"Define"** — give a precise definition. **"Discuss"** — explore the discipline: what it covers, why it arose, what it aims at and why it matters. **"Differentiate"** — give clear differences on named points, ideally in a table, with examples. "Discuss" carries the most marks.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Sex and gender
**Sex** means body differences between females and males. **Gender** means the roles, behaviour and power that society builds on those differences.

#### ii) Gender as power
The historian **Joan Wallach Scott** (==1986==) called gender "**a primary way of signifying relationships of power**" — one of the main ways a society shows who rules whom.

#### iii) The argument
**Gender Studies** is the discipline that studies this power. It grew out of **Women's Studies** and differs from it mainly by studying gender *relations* rather than women alone. Yet the two are best seen as **partners in one project**.

### 2. Defining the discipline

::: define Gender Studies | the working definition
The academic field that studies **how society makes femininity and masculinity**, how this **shares out power**, money and chances between women, men and gender-diverse people, and how gender **mixes with** class, religion, race and other identities.
-> Four key ideas: gender is made; it is about power; the field uses many disciplines; gender overlaps with other identities.
:::

It is a **discipline** because it has its own departments, degrees, journals, ideas (patriarchy, gender roles, intersectionality) and methods. Its research methods include surveys, interviews, life histories and the analysis of laws, texts and budgets. It is **multidisciplinary** because it borrows from sociology, psychology, anthropology, history, economics, law and literature.

### 3. The scope of the discipline

Gender Studies covers every area where gender shapes life:
- **identity and the body** — how people become women, men or otherwise;
- **family and home** — marriage, care, inheritance, violence at home;
- **work and economy** — paid and unpaid work, the pay gap;
- **politics and law** — voting, representation, rights;
- **culture** — religion, language, media, education;
- **global changes** — globalisation, migration, climate change.

It studies **women, men and gender-diverse people**, and the ways gender overlaps with class, religion, ethnicity, disability and location.

### 4. Why the discipline arose

#### i) Men were the measure
Knowledge was **androcentric** — it treated men as the normal human. **Carol Gilligan** (==1982==) showed that theories of moral growth built on boys ranked girls' "**ethic of care**" as weaker.

#### ii) Women's work was invisible
**Marilyn Waring** (*If Women Counted*, ==1988==) showed that **GDP ignores unpaid work** — cooking, cleaning and caring.

#### iii) Adding women was not enough
"**Add women and stir**" — adding a chapter on women — left the old frameworks untouched. A field was needed that questioned them, and later studied **men and relations** too.

### 5. Aims and features

#### i) Aims
To **make the invisible visible**, to **explain** inequality (not just describe it), to **question** what looks natural, to **guide policy** and to **empower**.

#### ii) Features
It is **committed** to justice (joining study with action, called **praxis**); it asks **who makes knowledge**; and it is **intersectional** — it sees that a poor village woman and a rich city woman face different lives.

#### iii) Key ideas
The discipline's key ideas include **patriarchy** (a system of male power), **gender roles** (expected behaviour), **socialisation** (learning roles), **intersectionality** (overlapping disadvantages) and **empowerment** (gaining the power to choose).

### 6. Significance, especially for Pakistan

::: pakistan | Why it matters here
Pakistan joined **CEDAW** in ==1996== and promised **SDG 5** (gender equality) by ==2030==. It ranked ==143rd of 145== in the Global Gender Gap Report 2026, and only ==24.4%== of working-age women are in the labour force (LFS 2024–25). Understanding these gaps, and putting the laws on harassment (2010), honour crimes (2016) and violence at home into effect, needs gender expertise.
:::

### 7. Women's Studies

#### i) Origin
**Women's Studies** began in the USA as "**the academic arm of the women's movement**": a course at **Cornell** (==1969==), the first programme at **San Diego State** (==1970==) and the **National Women's Studies Association** (==1977==).

#### ii) Features
It made women the **subject** of study, the **knowers** whose experience counts, and the **beneficiaries** of research.

#### iii) The shift to Gender Studies
From the 1980s, the idea of **gender** (Oakley ==1972==; Rubin ==1975==), **men's studies** (Connell ==1987==) and **queer theory** (about ==1990==) widened many programmes into Gender Studies.

#### iv) The same path in Pakistan
Pakistan's centres of ==1989== began as "**Women's Studies**" centres; most have since renamed themselves "**Gender Studies**" departments — the same shift, a generation later.

### 8. The differences

::: table | Women's Studies and Gender Studies compared.
@widths 2.6,5.7,5.7
Point | Women's Studies | Gender Studies
Subject | Women's lives, history and work | Relations between women, men and gender-diverse people
Key idea | "Woman" and women's oppression | Gender as made by society and as power
Origin | Women's movement, late 1960s | Gender theory, men's studies, queer theory, 1980s–90s
Aim | Make women visible; end subordination | Explain the whole gender system
Theories | Liberal, radical, socialist feminism | Adds constructionism, queer theory, intersectionality
Risk | Treating all women as one group | Letting women disappear again
:::

::: fig venn | What the two fields share, and what is distinctive to each.
@centre Shared: feminist roots; aim of equality
Women's Studies: women at the centre; women's voices
Gender Studies: gender relations; men and sexualities
:::

### 9. Examples

#### i) Honour killing
Women's Studies centres the **victims** — their families' accounts and the failure of police to protect them. Gender Studies adds the **killers and the system**: how ***ghairat*** (honour) is taught to boys, how families bargain over "forgiveness", and how the law let killers escape until the ==2016== amendment.

#### ii) Maternal health
Women's Studies records women's **experience** of childbirth. Gender Studies also studies **who decides** — husbands and mothers-in-law who control clinic visits, and male-led health budgets.

#### iii) Politics
Women's Studies counts **women voters and legislators** and the barriers they face. Gender Studies also studies **how male party leaders choose candidates**, and how a political culture of force and money keeps women out.

### 10. Conclusion
Gender Studies is the discipline that studies how gender is made and how it shares out power, across every part of life and every branch of knowledge. It differs from Women's Studies in its subject and key idea — relations rather than women alone — but not in its commitment. As **Tania Modleski** warned in *Feminism Without Women* (==1991==), the move to "gender" must not make women invisible again. In Pakistan, where women's basic health, education and work still need urgent attention, the two fields work best as **partners**. Women's Studies keeps the spotlight on women's lives; Gender Studies explains the system around them.

## Why this answer scores
- All three command words — **define, discuss, differentiate** — have their own headings.
- The definition is **simple and precise**, and its key ideas are explained.
- "Discuss" is developed through **scope, origins, aims and significance**, with named evidence.
- A **table**, a **Venn diagram** and **two examples** make the differences concrete.
- The conclusion takes a **clear view** (partnership) and applies it to Pakistan.
